WEBVTT

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.380
A team can get stuck asking the same questions.

00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:06.330
A lesson from another field can open a different

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:06.960
door.

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.230
Northstar Studio is building Project Beacon.

00:00:10.310 --> 00:00:12.990
Its five teammates have six weeks to learn who

00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:15.640
needs it, what to build, and how to work

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:16.161
together.

00:00:16.221 --> 00:00:19.771
Maya chooses lessons from the school of hard books.

00:00:19.831 --> 00:00:22.101
She gives the session a name and adds the

00:00:22.211 --> 00:00:23.431
project brief.

00:00:23.491 --> 00:00:27.291
The team will borrow ideas from architecture, cooking, and

00:00:27.291 --> 00:00:28.181
design.

00:00:28.271 --> 00:00:30.481
The lesson starts the conversation.

00:00:30.701 --> 00:00:32.972
Their own situation gives it a purpose.

00:00:33.032 --> 00:00:36.452
The first lesson is about thresholds, the space between

00:00:36.452 --> 00:00:37.982
one state and another.

00:00:38.032 --> 00:00:40.582
How could that help a new Beacon customer?

00:00:40.672 --> 00:00:45.222
Priya suggests trying three sample requests before connecting real

00:00:45.222 --> 00:00:45.942
tools.

00:00:45.992 --> 00:00:50.172
Other teammates propose a sandbox, a small import and

00:00:50.172 --> 00:00:53.004
clear boundaries between experiments and production.

00:00:53.044 --> 00:00:55.084
Everyone has time to write.

00:00:55.084 --> 00:00:57.134
Then Maya opens voting.

00:00:57.164 --> 00:01:00.314
The team reads the ideas without names and picks

00:01:00.314 --> 00:01:02.924
a first, second, and third choice.

00:01:02.964 --> 00:01:05.654
When all five votes are in, she reveals the

00:01:05.744 --> 00:01:06.474
results.

00:01:06.644 --> 00:01:09.674
The votes show which ideas drew support.

00:01:09.704 --> 00:01:11.841
They give the discussion a place to begin.

00:01:11.901 --> 00:01:14.231
Workie reads the answers in light of the brief

00:01:14.451 --> 00:01:17.051
and draws out points to discuss.

00:01:17.101 --> 00:01:19.211
Priya adds a useful check.

00:01:19.211 --> 00:01:22.971
A smooth demo does not prove customers need Beacon.

00:01:23.021 --> 00:01:25.791
She will test the sample workspace with two product

00:01:25.791 --> 00:01:28.491
leads and record where they hesitate.

00:01:28.541 --> 00:01:30.486
That feedback stays with the round.

00:01:30.546 --> 00:01:33.756
Next comes a lesson from cooking, taste as you

00:01:34.336 --> 00:01:34.356
go.

00:01:34.406 --> 00:01:38.446
The team proposes weekly customer tests and an experiment

00:01:38.506 --> 00:01:38.906
log.

00:01:39.076 --> 00:01:42.206
A design lesson asks what they can remove.

00:01:42.306 --> 00:01:45.696
Their answers narrow the first prototype to one customer

00:01:45.696 --> 00:01:49.096
group, one painful workflow and one clear path to

00:01:49.136 --> 00:01:49.921
a decision.

00:01:49.961 --> 00:01:53.311
Maya opens rounds, then the session report.

00:01:53.351 --> 00:01:56.971
All three lessons are here with the responses, votes,

00:01:57.051 --> 00:01:59.951
analysis, and follow-up comments.

00:01:59.991 --> 00:02:02.101
The team can trace an idea back to the

00:02:02.111 --> 00:02:03.811
question that prompted it.

00:02:03.851 --> 00:02:06.531
She saves the report for one year and downloads

00:02:06.921 --> 00:02:07.411
a copy.

00:02:07.464 --> 00:02:11.174
Use these lessons to explore an opportunity, challenge a

00:02:11.174 --> 00:02:13.354
habit, or find a better question.

00:02:13.644 --> 00:02:17.024
Your team supplies the ideas and decides what to

00:02:17.024 --> 00:02:17.674
test.

00:02:17.784 --> 00:02:19.787
What could a different field help you see?
